<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sherburn-in-Elmet</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sherburn-in-Elmet is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-yorkshire">North Yorkshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, between Leeds and Selby. Its name preserves that of the early British kingdom of Elmet, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was an important estate and residence of the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sherburn-in-elmet.jpg" alt="Sherburn-in-Elmet" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gordon Kneale Brooke · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sherburn_in_Elmet_Village_-_geograph.org.uk_-_104998.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sherburn stands on a low hill of Permian limestone that juts into the valley of the River Ouse, west of Selby and south of Tadcaster. Its name comes from the Old English scir, bright or pure, and burn, a stream or spring, perhaps for the clear water of the hill beside the muddy streams of the plain below; the earliest record of it, Scyreburnan, dates from 963. The ridge is still a source of clear water, for the brewers of Tadcaster among others.</p><p>A Roman road from Castleford to Tadcaster and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> ran along the ridge, on the line of today's Low Street and Finkle Hill, though little sign of Roman settlement has been found. Elmet, the little-understood post-Roman Brittonic kingdom around what is now Leeds, is also recalled in the names of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barwick-in-elmet">Barwick-in-Elmet</a> and Scholes-in-Elmet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Beside All Saints' Church an earthwork marks Hall Garth, a high-status dwelling that King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Athelstan</a> gave, with the manor of Cawood, to the Archbishops of York after his victory over the Scots and Norse at Brunanburh in 937. It is sometimes wrongly called Athelstan's Palace and cannot be dated with certainty to the time before Elmet was absorbed into Northumbria in 616/7. The church, a Grade I listed building on the site of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> one, has a 12th-century nave and north aisle; Anthony Salvin restored its east end in 1857.</p><p>Local legend holds that Edward IV watched the Battle of Towton in 1461 from the church tower, but the main fighting, three to four miles away, could not have been seen clearly from a tower then lower than it is today. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> the Royalists garrisoned the town for Charles I. On 15 October 1645 a Cavalier force under Lord Digby and Sir Marmaduke Langdale defeated the Parliamentarian garrison, until Colonel Copley's counterattack routed it: the last significant Royalist force in the North during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-english-civil-war">First English Civil War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sherburn in Elmet is a civil parish of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-yorkshire">North Yorkshire</a>. It belonged to Selby District from 1974 to 2023 and is now administered by the unitary North Yorkshire Council. The 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 8,572 people, and its railway station has services to York, Hull and Sheffield.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sherburn in Elmet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It is remembered in place names such as <b>Sherburn-in-Elmet</b> and Barwick-in-Elmet, and a 6th-century memorial stone at Llanaelhaearn in Gwynedd names a man of Elmet.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-elmet">Kingdom of Elmet</a></li></ul></section>
